WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.405
Hi, I'm Muffin!

00:00:02.405 --> 00:00:06.494
cua-s1-forms fills in forms with a tiny model, trained from scratch.

00:00:06.494 --> 00:00:07.981
Here's how it chooses.

00:00:09.517 --> 00:00:11.422
The idea: score each option alone.

00:00:11.422 --> 00:00:15.549
The head outputs one number, so the option list is data, not weights.

00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:24.094
The form and document go through a tiny byte-level encoder, two layers deep.

00:00:24.094 --> 00:00:25.874
The options are encoded separately.

00:00:26.294 --> 00:00:29.853
Each option becomes a query against the context, and pulls out

00:00:29.853 --> 00:00:31.147
its own attended vector.

00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:34.592
A shared dot product turns each pair into one logit.

00:00:34.592 --> 00:00:36.407
The head's output dimension is one.

00:00:36.827 --> 00:00:40.208
Softmax runs over however many options the caller sent.

00:00:40.208 --> 00:00:43.965
Send two, send sixteen: the parameter count does not move.

00:00:44.385 --> 00:00:47.842
Notice what's missing: no term compares one option with another.

00:00:49.317 --> 00:00:53.026
The dataset is entirely synthetic, built by three generators.

00:00:53.446 --> 00:00:55.620
A random form of two to sixteen fields.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.545
A random synthetic person.

00:00:58.749 --> 00:01:02.430
And a document padded with look-alikes, like email against street address, so

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.578
the model must read the whole label.

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:08.835
Splits are disjoint by form, so a test form never shows up in training.

00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:13.656
The other family lists options as letters in the prompt and

00:01:13.656 --> 00:01:14.901
reads the letter tokens.

00:01:14.901 --> 00:01:16.768
Each letter has its own prior.

00:01:17.188 --> 00:01:20.739
The scalar scorer has no letters and no positions, so order

00:01:20.739 --> 00:01:22.030
is not even expressible.

00:01:23.425 --> 00:01:28.611
Reverse the options for openjev, a letter readout, and ten of thirty-six decisions change.

00:01:31.808 --> 00:01:35.680
So one number per option makes the list data, and its order irrelevant.

00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:43.220
To recap: a tiny encoder, one logit per option, and no letters

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.742
for order to cling to.

00:01:45.162 --> 00:01:47.245
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:47.245 --> 00:01:47.840
I'm Muffin.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:48.138
Bye!
